To create a new line within a cell in a LaTeX table, you can use the `\\` command. This is a special command in LaTeX that tells the compiler to start a new line.

Here's an example of how to use it:

```latex
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|}
\hline
Cell 1 & Cell 2 \\ \cline{2-2}
Cell 3 & Cell 4 \\ \cline{2-2}
Cell 5 & Cell 6 \\ \cline{2-2}
Cell 7 & Cell 8 \\ \cline{2-2}
\end{tabular}
```

However, if you want to create a new line within a cell, you can use the `\\` command inside the cell. Here's an example:

```latex
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|}
\hline
Cell 1 \\ 
Line 2 of Cell 1 & Cell 2 \\ \hline
\end{tabular}
```

In this example, the `\\` command is used to create a new line within the first cell.

Alternatively, you can use the `newline` command, which is a more explicit way to create a new line within a cell:

```latex
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|}
\hline
Cell 1 \newline Line 2 of Cell 1 & Cell 2 \\ \hline
\end{tabular}
```

Both of these methods will produce the same result: a new line within the cell.

